#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "c1924df7-205f-512e-b024-72f5a654f062" :question/math-academy-id "e-9560" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y )\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad\n\ty = 4\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-16(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548269 :question-generator/id #uuid "10a95066-ed8d-5d70-9389-6ee466552a64" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186179744.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab07d163-0625-52d3-bb7a-5ead102e3c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177844} {:answer/id #uuid "15190a02-e5af-5be2-8697-97e71684c08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177845} {:answer/id #uuid "2605ce5a-8cc3-5767-bd42-b4df2fc57fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177846} {:answer/id #uuid "6f00d621-c436-5bd5-8625-07e82e475235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177847} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdec857-84ed-52dd-8971-cb87df00b2a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177848}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d25cf8ea-2732-5072-883d-71745e726402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177843}] :question/id #uuid "d6daea5f-9d1c-5bf1-b6af-12c3102e71d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126717" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = yi - xj$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t) = 5\\cos ti + 5\\sin tj$ for $t ∈ [0, 2π)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfab73e-541e-5dea-a884-264b4bd8ec97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177913} {:answer/id #uuid "1d03d1d4-83e8-5434-ba88-654e772f6034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539535} {:answer/id #uuid "2bedcbb9-3a20-535c-92e0-bda037bb0531" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539560} {:answer/id #uuid "94ef6336-a885-5e0f-9339-fb9e597d51dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539726} {:answer/id #uuid "c61c8d76-f473-524c-b92c-3be06cc40c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d03d1d4-83e8-5434-ba88-654e772f6034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539535} :answer-field/id #uuid "dab28699-4fc7-5cb2-8f84-87f7666ba43c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4edd3b9a-070a-593a-8f84-1b14657d2c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-127055" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x+y=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y-x=3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1451c613-e020-5234-9207-ca8e2b426177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260055} {:answer/id #uuid "545e7de3-77c0-5001-a9e8-6b1adc5f1466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260589} {:answer/id #uuid "78acc197-697f-5085-b977-43333a81c4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260932} {:answer/id #uuid "7b79330c-376e-54f4-b1b3-7e784422cfed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260951} {:answer/id #uuid "a87d06f4-05e6-5e25-a200-9da07f1daa38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1451c613-e020-5234-9207-ca8e2b426177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260055} :answer-field/id #uuid "473e948e-e7b1-5fb0-bd43-b3e933074eaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6160f48-7946-52fa-bf89-f2b84f79ba1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127060" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=4{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1}-16{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2873f634-c695-583b-b477-95e3c1ffc07a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260232} {:answer/id #uuid "4babd7c8-f323-5126-a6c0-63a9f81a9e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260524} {:answer/id #uuid "66277e5d-0782-5296-aac5-7543f0ac4723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260762} {:answer/id #uuid "86598988-6527-5185-a16f-ee494f22fee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261043} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bc2f7b-8b39-5063-8765-91cf86585fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6bc2f7b-8b39-5063-8765-91cf86585fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261329} :answer-field/id #uuid "30577348-fd2b-554c-96e0-1728db3f4497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "697f638b-1499-58a9-805f-6f4605d73510" :question/math-academy-id "q-127077" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =60\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2ab957-0579-5e99-8c2c-f53cec1a64ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260254} {:answer/id #uuid "515d246a-bf62-5921-88e9-813966854ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260564} {:answer/id #uuid "681d213e-13d8-58eb-9b2e-feeb4d760e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260785} {:answer/id #uuid "787af263-8fcd-544e-a39d-804db798c96f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260930} {:answer/id #uuid "95210164-0d4e-5860-ac8f-9ab7e9038d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "515d246a-bf62-5921-88e9-813966854ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260564} :answer-field/id #uuid "b00b1074-3f5c-50fa-8cd5-1bc5332b0ac4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "610929b6-feab-56c5-9108-52a1535e1a43" :question/math-academy-id "q-127081" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i+9{x}^{3}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =9{x}^{3}=9{cos}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=u,du=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(9{cos}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{1}^{1}(u+{u}^{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6893a418-d322-5ab1-83ac-21e6380ffd8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260788} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1a6f11-e4dc-58f0-b3b9-20f5896018bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261107} {:answer/id #uuid "ad14b8d3-dc2a-51ae-93a4-3065094d2fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261390} {:answer/id #uuid "c8457952-5981-52f2-8dd0-d726a811dc80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261647} {:answer/id #uuid "e9652b71-0ef3-57bb-b58d-c0c40977ff4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9652b71-0ef3-57bb-b58d-c0c40977ff4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261917} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4fee625-b03f-59fc-b891-1aff5cd98622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9148e698-2674-5a60-a839-9ed2c4895150" :question/math-academy-id "q-127139" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=49{cos}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(49{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(1)\\cdot (-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98{cos}^{3}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-98{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }98\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{0}-98{u}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =(98\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8337cc5b-cbe0-5afa-9274-103e3f498697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261015} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8c2924-c012-51ca-aa9f-ebee29edb99a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261114} {:answer/id #uuid "9958d4dc-9c0d-556d-9236-c69dc7ace6dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261204} {:answer/id #uuid "9c54fcc6-cb8b-5805-9834-9c56673a0e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261228} {:answer/id #uuid "b08ecf9c-f351-5fc2-9328-9e808bafe62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b08ecf9c-f351-5fc2-9328-9e808bafe62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261434} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2ba8e33-8924-5bcc-b848-378e5d728fa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc62686d-15ed-5e7b-a83e-66fbf5f6c46f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127140" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=4x\\,i+4y\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4x=4(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =4y=4(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }192({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }192\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =192t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =192(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =384\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093b5f1d-7084-5cca-af48-56a7c276ea5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421730} {:answer/id #uuid "1755e313-29bb-5235-930c-5480eea8e580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421732} {:answer/id #uuid "2a42ddb5-8533-50f3-9230-498c83bae1a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421735} {:answer/id #uuid "93070110-72d1-593c-aaa7-f43291b04560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421745} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b8c70b-0bf4-5466-85d5-56e8bfa6fc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25e}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "093b5f1d-7084-5cca-af48-56a7c276ea5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421730} :answer-field/id #uuid "452bd17e-660a-568a-8108-39d6d0770162" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90df2f56-7b61-5a49-aa6d-4d0c58b35b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-127062" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={e}^{y}={e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={e}^{x}={e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitutions $u=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,du=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt$ and $v=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,dv=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}{e}^{u}\\,du-{\\int }_{1}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29d3016e-ea1f-57be-ad3f-89bd52e2b595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421733} {:answer/id #uuid "29d80bea-8d5f-5d6e-9d57-a11322c402b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421734} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd76549-ed5c-561a-a07f-a7506fbab6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421736} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf1e009-304c-5363-9a50-24170560d53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421738} {:answer/id #uuid "eb22ea92-b3d9-59e8-9fe5-49b32e301c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cf1e009-304c-5363-9a50-24170560d53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421738} :answer-field/id #uuid "8411ac91-aa07-508a-9904-aa0a020877bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2007897-e589-5e81-a8ff-d3a65c150dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-127082" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+(x+y)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x+y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+15{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }15\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =15t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =15(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =30\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}]}
